WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.902 align:start size:94% position:6%
-One of the reasons
that makes sense to me,

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:04.571
both that you would write
and want to direct it,

00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:06.072 size:78% position:39%
is it´s so personal,
and one of the things

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:07.607 align:start size:94% position:6%
that was interesting to me
is you obviously set it closer

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:09.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
to when you grew up?
-Yeah.

00:00:09.042 --> 00:00:13.213
And so no cellphones, which was
such a fun thing to watch,

00:00:13.213 --> 00:00:16.182
a high school movie where
cellphones is not a plot point.

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:18.651 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I know. You could not know
where someone was.

00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:20.186 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Which is amazing.

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:22.689
-Yeah, and so much more helpful
for storytelling that you --

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:23.990 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes. Yeah.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.192 align:start size:94% position:6%
For any story, it´s good
to not know where anybody is.

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:27.894 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Laughing ] Yeah.
-It´s a good thing.

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:29.562 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, because usually
huge plot points

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:30.897 align:start size:94% position:6%
are either
that person showing up or --

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:32.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:36.436
-So, no, it was -- I actually --
I like on my film sets

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:38.738 align:start size:94% position:6%
and now I can say that,
my one film set,

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:41.875 align:start size:94% position:6%
but -- I like -- nobody has
cellphones anyway.

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:43.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don´t like having cellphones
on set

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:46.479
because I think it helps
the actors to concentrate.

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:49.849
But I had a young cast,
like, you know, 18 to 22.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.217
There were a lot of young people

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.219 size:81% position:41%
and everyone was like,
"Good luck with that."

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:55.155 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, "they´ll never give up
those phones,"

00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:58.458 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I sold it to them as being
about it´s an acting thing.

00:00:58.458 --> 00:01:00.360 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, and they bought in?
-Yeah.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.662 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I was like,
"This is for your acting."

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:03.463 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:03.463 --> 00:01:05.131
And they were like, "Oh, great!"

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:07.400
-You also -- I thought this was
really cool.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.036
You have people -- you had
your crew wear name tags?

00:01:10.036 --> 00:01:10.737 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:11.838
-And not just their name.

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:13.740 align:start size:94% position:6%
There would be
a different question --

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.208
A different answer every day?

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:16.709
-Yeah, well, there was a --

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:19.079
I had everyone wear name tags
because I feel like for actors,

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:21.681
especially, if you´re coming in
for like a day or two days,

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:23.316 align:start size:94% position:6%
it´s hard to get
to know everyone,

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:24.684
and it made everybody a person.

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:27.020 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I had a name tag.
Everybody had a name tag.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.156
And then our -- there was a P.A.
who was wonderful, Dayna,

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:32.225
who came up with the question of
the day every day.

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:33.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it would be a trivia
question

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:35.895 align:start size:94% position:6%
or sort of like
a conversation starter,

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:38.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
like, "what was
your go-to karaoke song?"

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:41.334 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or "what what would you do
if you didn´t do this?"

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:43.069 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or "what is a movie that you
know you should like

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:44.571 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you don´t?"

00:01:44.571 --> 00:01:47.807
-What was a movie that you know
you should like but you don´t?

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:49.976 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"Breakfast at Tiffany´s."
-Oh, wow!

00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:51.311 align:start size:94% position:6%
It´s so beautiful,

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:54.114 align:start size:94% position:6%
but there´s something about it
that gives me the creeps.

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:55.448
And I don´t Know what it is.

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:57.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, Audrey Hepburn is
glorious and I --

00:01:57.517 --> 00:01:59.018 align:start size:94% position:6%
but it´s just true --
-Yeah.

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:00.453 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Light laughter ]

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.789 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Look, if you´re creeped out,
you´re creeped out.

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:04.991 align:start size:94% position:6%
That´s just -- there´s no
getting around that.

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:06.359
-I really think that is true.

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:09.062 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Another thing that strikes me
as it was very personal

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:10.663 align:start size:94% position:6%
was the music choices.
-Yes.

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:11.998
-For those who don´t know,

00:02:11.998 --> 00:02:14.400 align:start size:94% position:6%
when there´s a song
you really want in a movie,

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.369 align:start size:94% position:6%
you have to get permission
to use it.

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:19.239 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you personally reached out
to some people via letters?

00:02:19.239 --> 00:02:20.607
-Yes, I wrote some letters.

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:22.108
-Like, for example, there´s --

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:24.344 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I do feel like
these songs were very --

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:26.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
they kind of
had to be those songs.

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:28.548
-Yes, they had to -- I had
written them into the script.

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:30.984 align:start size:94% position:6%
They were very specifically
those songs.

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:35.355
-And so you wrote
Alanis Morissette a nice letter.

00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:37.223 align:start size:94% position:6%
And what were some of
your selling points to her?

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:40.326
-Well, so, "I have been a fan of
yours my whole life.

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:41.961
The very first cassette tape

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:44.364 align:start size:94% position:6%
I ever bought was
´Jagged Little Pill.´

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:47.667
I would listen to ´Perfect´ over
and over in my living room,

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.135 size:81% position:41%
memorizing the lyrics.

00:02:49.135 --> 00:02:51.905
I remember bringing that same
cassette to a sleepover party

00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:54.474
where we all played our favorite
songs for each other.

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:56.809 align:start size:94% position:6%
I played ´Hand in my Pocket´
because my mother told me

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:58.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
that ´Perfect´ might be
a little too heavy

00:02:58.878 --> 00:02:59.612 align:start size:94% position:6%
for a fifth grade
slumber party."

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:01.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:04.751
"I saw the film ´Dogma´ because
I read that you played God,

00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:06.653 align:start size:94% position:6%
which seemed totally fitting
to me."

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:09.255
-Yeah, and you think "Breakfast
at Tiffany´s" is creepy?

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:11.057 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, I know.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:13.726 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is an example of
my creepiness.

00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:15.562
-No, but this was really great.

00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:18.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
Wait, you have to --
You wrote Dave Matthews?

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:18.965 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.

00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:20.833
-And obviously you´ve moved on.

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:24.070 align:start size:94% position:6%
I like that you´ve moved on
from cassettes to CDs.

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:24.971 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you knew you couldn´t
tell him

00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:26.439
it was his first cassette?

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:30.577
-Yeah, this is -- I say, "These
were my very first CD purchases.

00:03:30.577 --> 00:03:31.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:35.348
And I made my dad take me to see
you and your band perform

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:37.684
when you came to Sacramento."

00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:42.121 align:start size:94% position:6%
So -- and I said -- I said
that the song "Crash into Me,"

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.057
which is the song in the movie,

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:48.228
was the most romantic song ever,
underlined.

00:03:48.228 --> 00:03:49.562 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:51.764 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I -- And I was --
And then I said,

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:54.434 size:88% position:44%
"Thank you so much for
considering the request.

00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:57.670
I wish I could go back in time
and tell my 16-year-old self

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.239
that this moment was coming.

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:01.741 align:start size:94% position:6%
She´d never believe me,
but truth be told,

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:04.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
my 32-year-old self
can hardly believe it either."

00:04:04.544 --> 00:04:06.045 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:07.580 size:84% position:42%
-I like that the moment
you can´t believe is

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:08.848 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you´re writing him
a letter.

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:14.087 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I know. I can´t believe it.
Can´t believe my letter.

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:16.155
-And Justin Timberlake,
as well, and they all said yes.

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:17.523 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes!
Oh, the Justin Timberlake --

00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:18.992 align:start size:94% position:6%
That has
a really awkward beginning.

00:04:18.992 --> 00:04:20.326 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, yeah, let´s --
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:21.494 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I got it here.

00:04:21.494 --> 00:04:22.729 align:start size:94% position:6%
I like that you´re embracing
your awkwardness.

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:25.098 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Okay, so,
"Dear Mr. Timberlake --

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:26.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:29.269 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, what can I say?
You´re Justin Timberlake."

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:31.070 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.873 align:start size:94% position:6%
"You were the soundtrack
to my adolescence.

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:37.043
Your rise corresponded exactly
with my very awkward puberty."

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:38.111 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:39.846 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And then it just gets worse.
-Yeah.

00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:41.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:41.147 --> 00:04:43.516
Yeah. "I pretty much wouldn´t be
an adult without you."

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:46.085 size:88% position:44%
That´s the nicest thing
you could say to anyone.

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:49.289
That´s heartbreak-- when your
parents -- when they hear that,

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:50.657 size:84% position:42%
they´ll be heartbroken.

00:04:50.657 --> 00:04:53.393
-They´ll be like, "This was
Justin Timberlake´s doing?

00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:55.361 align:start size:94% position:6%
What were we doing
all that time?"

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:56.796
-Well, I´m glad it paid off

00:04:56.796 --> 00:04:59.165
because it´s hard now to imagine
the film without those songs.

00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:00.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:03.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And congratulations on making
such a beautiful film.

00:05:03.036 --> 00:05:04.604
-Thank you, thank you.
-Greta Gerwig, everybody.

